---
import PaperLayout from '../../layouts/PaperLayout.astro';

const authors = [
  { name: 'Seunghyeon Seo', href: 'https://shawn615.github.io/', affiliation: 'Seoul National University' },
  { name: 'Donghoon Han', href: 'https://scholar.google.com/citations?hl=en&user=BKlC7TQAAAAJ', affiliation: 'Seoul National University', equal: true, me: true },
  { name: 'Yeonjin Chang', href: 'https://yeonjin-chang.github.io/', affiliation: 'Seoul National University', equal: true },
  { name: 'Nojun Kwak', href: 'http://mipal.snu.ac.kr/index.php/Nojun_Kwak', affiliation: 'Seoul National University' },
];

const links = [
  { label: 'arXiv', href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.08788', meta: '2302.08788' },
  { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/shawn615/MixNeRF' },
  { label: 'Video', href: 'https://youtu.be/PXljJordbFk' },
  { label: 'Project (canonical)', href: 'https://shawn615.github.io/mixnerf' },
];
---

<PaperLayout
  title="MixNeRF"
  subtitle="Modeling a Ray with Mixture Density for Novel View Synthesis from Sparse Inputs"
  venue="CVPR 2023"
  authors={authors}
  links={links}
  description="MixNeRF: efficient few-shot novel view synthesis by modeling each ray with a mixture density model, with ray depth as an auxiliary task."
>
  <section>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/teaser.png" alt="MixNeRF teaser" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <h2>Abstract</h2>
    <p>
      Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) deliver state-of-the-art novel-view synthesis but
      degrade severely without dense training views. Prior fixes lean on heavy extra
      training resources, undermining the efficiency premise of sparse-input NeRF. We
      propose <strong>MixNeRF</strong>, an effective training strategy that models each
      ray with a mixture density model. MixNeRF estimates the joint distribution of
      RGB along ray samples as a mixture of distributions, and introduces ray depth
      estimation as a useful training objective tightly correlated with 3D scene
      geometry. With regenerated blending weights from estimated depth, robustness to
      color and viewpoint shifts further improves. MixNeRF outperforms prior
      state-of-the-art across standard benchmarks while being more training- and
      inference-efficient.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Video</h2>
    <figure class="video-embed">
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/PXljJordbFk"
        title="MixNeRF — CVPR 2023"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Modeling a ray with a mixture density model</h2>
    <p>
      MixNeRF estimates the per-pixel RGB distribution along ray samples as a
      mixture of component distributions. The conventional NeRF outputs at each
      sample serve as a location parameter and (via the density σ) as the mixing
      coefficient π; we additionally estimate a scale parameter β.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/mixture_1.png" alt="Mixture component definition" loading="lazy" style="max-width: 60%; margin: 0 auto;" />
    </figure>
    <p>The pdf of the mixture model is:</p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/mixture_2.png" alt="Mixture pdf" loading="lazy" style="max-width: 60%; margin: 0 auto;" />
    </figure>
    <p>The mixture coefficient is derived from the density output:</p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/mixture_3.png" alt="Mixture coefficient from density" loading="lazy" style="max-width: 60%; margin: 0 auto;" />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Depth estimation by mixture density</h2>
    <p>
      We propose ray depth estimation as an auxiliary task — defined as the length of
      the unnormalized ray-direction vector along ray samples — providing direct
      supervision for the mixture parameters tied to geometry.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/raydepth_1.png" alt="Mixture pdf for depth" loading="lazy" style="max-width: 60%; margin: 0 auto;" />
    </figure>
    <p>
      With π and β jointly optimized via depth and color supervision, MixNeRF
      becomes more robust to small geometric shifts. Estimated depth then regenerates
      blending weights — playing the role of pseudo-geometry for adjacent samples
      without extra training data:
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/raydepth_2.png" alt="Regenerated blending weights" loading="lazy" style="max-width: 60%; margin: 0 auto;" />
    </figure>
    <p>Color values along the ray are remodeled with the new mixing coefficients:</p>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/raydepth_3.png" alt="Final color modeling" loading="lazy" style="max-width: 60%; margin: 0 auto;" />
    </figure>
    <p>
      No extra inference of unseen views is required, so robustness is gained without
      meaningful computational overhead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why mixture density helps</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/weight_dist.png" alt="Weight distribution comparison" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <p>
      For unimodal cases (blue), mip-NeRF underestimates the mode and yields
      degenerate geometry. RegNeRF and MixNeRF recover unimodal weights, with
      MixNeRF producing a sharper mode that more closely matches the dense-view
      reference. For bimodal cases (red), only MixNeRF estimates the distribution
      successfully — directly improving geometry under limited views.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Depth map estimation</h2>
    <figure>
      <video
        src="/mixnerf/video/depth_regnerf_ours.mp4"
        playsinline
        autoplay
        loop
        muted
      ></video>
    </figure>
    <p>
      RegNeRF's smoothing prior collapses geometry. MixNeRF learns ray depth via the
      mixture density model — without smoothing across additional unseen rays — so
      depth maps come out cleaner and more precise.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Efficiency</h2>
    <figure>
      <img src="/mixnerf/img/efficiency_1.png" alt="Training-time vs. quality comparison" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>
    <p>
      DietNeRF trains in similar time but is significantly weaker in 3- and 6-view
      settings. Against RegNeRF, MixNeRF cuts per-scene training time by ~42% under
      matched view counts by eliminating extra inference for additional unseen rays.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Results</h2>

    <h3>DTU 3-view</h3>
    <div class="video-grid v3">
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_dtu_1.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_dtu_2.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_dtu_3.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
    </div>

    <h3>LLFF 3-view</h3>
    <div class="video-grid v2">
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_llff_1.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_llff_2.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
    </div>

    <h3>Blender 4-view</h3>
    <div class="video-grid v2">
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_blender_1.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
      <video src="/mixnerf/video/results_blender_2.mp4" playsinline autoplay loop muted></video>
    </div>
  </section>

